section='''<section><h2>Inspect database connections and datasets</h2><p>Open the intended project or cache and choose <strong>Database connections</strong> in the upper-right page selector. Select a connection, dataset, data source, transaction, session or named definition reference. Read its generation, source hash/line, masked configuration, outgoing references and Used by dependents in the labeled memo. No target database or driver is opened.</p><p>Filter by kind or text, including reference and reverse-dependency names. First page and Next page display 250 rows. Scroll the toolbar horizontally to reach the reference selector and <strong>Follow reference</strong>. Choose a Target or Used by entry, then Follow reference to open that exact row and page. Drag the divider below the list to adjust the evidence area and scroll the memo vertically.</p><p>Unique local, nested and qualified cross-module references are lexical candidates. Duplicate, absent, wrong-role and unsupported references remain unresolved; nil stays explicit. External definition contents, environment overrides, inherited defaults and runtime assignments remain unknown. Pascal-only declarations do not prove construction. An empty list does not prove the application never uses a database.</p><p>Endpoints, credentials and entire connection strings are masked. Only validated driver/protocol/dialect/charset/SSL identifiers, ports and booleans are shown. Connected=True is a serialized declaration, not a successful connection. Original source/cache files can still contain secrets. Do not put credentials into authored explanations. Full directions: <code>docs/guides/Database_Connections_Run_and_Test.txt</code>. Elevated developer checks: <code>tools/build-database-connections.cmd</code>.</p></section>'''
